  <main>

    <!-- HERO -->
    <section class="tech-hero">
      <div class="tech-hero-content">
        <span class="product-category">Technology</span>
        <h1 class="tech-hero-headline">Direct Conversion X-ray Imaging Technology</h1>
        <p class="tech-hero-sub">
          Athlos direct conversion sensors convert X-ray photons directly into electrical signal — without the scintillator light-spread stage used in conventional indirect detectors.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ANSWER BOX -->
    <section class="tech-section tech-section--light" id="definition">
      <div class="container container--narrow">
        <div class="tech-answer-box reveal">
          <p>
            <strong>Direct conversion X-ray imaging</strong> is a detector technology where X-ray photons are absorbed directly in a semiconductor material, such as CdTe or silicon, and converted into electrical charge. Unlike indirect conversion, it does not first convert X-rays into visible light in a scintillator. This removes the scintillator light-spread stage and helps preserve spatial accuracy, contrast and detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- WHAT IS DIRECT CONVERSION -->
        <div class="tech-block">
          <p class="section-eyebrow reveal">The Basics</p>
          <h2 class="section-headline reveal" id="what-is-direct-conversion-imaging">What is direct conversion X-ray imaging?</h2>
          <p class="tech-body reveal">
            In a direct conversion detector, the X-ray-to-signal pathway is short and localized. The process has five steps:
          </p>
          {#- shared gradient/filter defs for every dcs instance (kept
              visible at 0×0 — display:none would break url() refs in
              some browsers; same approach as the homepage dcc-defs) -#}
          <svg class="dcs-defs" width="0" height="0" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
            <defs>
              <linearGradient id="dcs-slab" x1="0" y1="0" x2="0" y2="1">
                <stop offset="0" stop-color="#ecf5fb"/>
                <stop offset="1" stop-color="#d4e5f0"/>
              </linearGradient>
              <linearGradient id="dcs-cmos" x1="0" y1="0" x2="0" y2="1">
                <stop offset="0" stop-color="#e9edf1"/>
                <stop offset="1" stop-color="#d8dfe5"/>
              </linearGradient>
              <radialGradient id="dcs-glow" cx="0.5" cy="0.5" r="0.5">
                <stop offset="0" stop-color="#ffffff" stop-opacity="0.95"/>
                <stop offset="0.4" stop-color="#7dbfe8" stop-opacity="0.7"/>
                <stop offset="1" stop-color="#43a1d8" stop-opacity="0"/>
              </radialGradient>
              <filter id="dcs-blur" x="-60%" y="-60%" width="220%" height="220%">
                <feGaussianBlur stdDeviation="4"/>
              </filter>
            </defs>
          </svg>
          <div class="tech-steps-layout">
            <ol class="tech-steps reveal" id="dcs-steps">
              <li data-step="1"><strong>An X-ray photon enters the detector material</strong> — a semiconductor such as cadmium telluride (CdTe) or crystalline silicon (Si).
                <span class="tech-step-mini">{{ dcsScene(1, "dcs--static") }}</span>
              </li>
              <li data-step="2"><strong>The photon is absorbed</strong> in the semiconductor through photoelectric interaction.
                <span class="tech-step-mini">{{ dcsScene(2, "dcs--static") }}</span>
              </li>
              <li data-step="3"><strong>The absorbed energy creates charge carriers</strong> — electron–hole pairs — in proportion to the photon's energy.
                <span class="tech-step-mini">{{ dcsScene(3, "dcs--static") }}</span>
              </li>
              <li data-step="4"><strong>An applied electric field drives the charge to local pixel electrodes</strong>, keeping the signal footprint close to the point of absorption.
                <span class="tech-step-mini">{{ dcsScene(4, "dcs--static") }}</span>
              </li>
              <li data-step="5"><strong>A CMOS/ASIC readout circuit, bonded directly to the detector material, converts the collected charge into a digital signal.</strong>
                <span class="tech-step-mini">{{ dcsScene(5, "dcs--static") }}</span>
              </li>
            </ol>
            {#- decorative — the five steps above carry the information -#}
            <div class="tech-steps-stage reveal" aria-hidden="true">
              {{ dcsScene(1, "dcs--stage") }}
            </div>
          </div>
          <script>
            // Sync the sticky illustration stage with the step the reader
            // is on (scroll position via IntersectionObserver, or hover).
            // The stage is decorative and defaults to step 1 without JS;
            // mobile thumbnails are static and need none of this.
            (function () {
              var stage = document.querySelector('.tech-steps-stage .dcs');
              var steps = document.querySelectorAll('#dcs-steps > li[data-step]');
              if (!stage || !steps.length) return;
              function setActive(n) {
                stage.setAttribute('data-active', String(n));
                Array.prototype.forEach.call(steps, function (li) {
                  li.classList.toggle('is-active', li.getAttribute('data-step') === String(n));
                });
              }
              setActive(1);
              Array.prototype.forEach.call(steps, function (li) {
                li.addEventListener('mouseenter', function () {
                  setActive(li.getAttribute('data-step'));
                });
              });
              if ('IntersectionObserver' in window) {
                var io = new IntersectionObserver(function (entries) {
                  entries.forEach(function (e) {
                    if (e.isIntersecting) setActive(e.target.getAttribute('data-step'));
                  });
                }, { rootMargin: '-45% 0px -45% 0px' });
                Array.prototype.forEach.call(steps, function (li) { io.observe(li); });
              }
            })();
          </script>
          <p class="tech-body reveal">
            No scintillator and no visible-light conversion step is required. Because the charge is collected by electrodes at the pixel where the photon was absorbed, direct conversion helps preserve a more localized signal path from photon to pixel — supporting sharpness, contrast and detail, depending on detector material, geometry and system design.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- DIRECT VS INDIRECT COMPARISON -->
    <section class="tech-section tech-section--dark" id="direct-vs-indirect">
      <div class="container">
        <p class="section-eyebrow section-eyebrow--light reveal">Comparison</p>
        <h2 class="section-headline section-headline--light reveal">Direct vs. indirect conversion: what is the difference?</h2>
        <p class="tech-body tech-body--dark reveal">
          Most digital X-ray detectors in use today are <em>indirect</em>: they first convert X-rays into visible light in a scintillator layer (such as CsI or GOS), and a photodiode array then converts that light into electrical signal. Direct conversion detectors skip the light stage entirely.
        </p>
        <div class="tech-table-wrap reveal">
          <table class="tech-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Feature</th>
                <th scope="col">Indirect conversion (scintillator)</th>
                <th scope="col">Direct conversion</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Signal path</th>
                <td>X-ray → visible light → electrical charge</td>
                <td>X-ray → electrical charge</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Intermediate conversion stage</th>
                <td>Scintillator (e.g. CsI, GOS) converts X-rays to visible light</td>
                <td>None — absorption and charge generation happen in the same semiconductor layer</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Main spread mechanism</th>
                <td>Visible light can spread laterally in the scintillator before reaching the photodiode</td>
                <td>Charge is drawn to local pixel electrodes by an electric field; lateral spread is smaller, though charge sharing between neighbouring pixels can still occur</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Detector material</th>
                <td>Scintillator + photodiode array (a-Si, CMOS or CCD)</td>
                <td>Semiconductor absorber: CdTe, CdZnTe, Si or a-Se</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Readout architecture</th>
                <td>Photodiode/TFT or CMOS behind the scintillator</td>
                <td>CMOS/ASIC readout bonded directly to the semiconductor (e.g. bump-bonded hybrid)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Signal localization</th>
                <td>Footprint broadened by light spread; columnar CsI mitigates but does not remove this stage</td>
                <td>More localized signal path from absorption point to pixel electrode</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Image sharpness</th>
                <td>Constrained by the light-spread stage, particularly at high spatial frequencies</td>
                <td>Helps preserve sharpness and MTF at high spatial frequencies, depending on material, pixel geometry and system design</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Typical strengths</th>
                <td>Mature, cost-effective, widely available; scales to very large flat-panel areas</td>
                <td>Sharpness, contrast resolution and dose-efficiency potential; compact sensor structure</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Typical limitations</th>
                <td>Light spread, fill-factor and conversion losses; each extra stage can add noise</td>
                <td>High-quality semiconductor material (especially CdTe) is harder to produce; requires careful contact engineering, calibration and material know-how</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Athlos relevance</th>
                <td>—</td>
                <td>Athlos builds exclusively direct conversion sensors: CdTe-CMOS (UFS, Industrial IP67 TDI) and Si-CMOS (DC-Air®)</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="tech-body tech-body--dark reveal">
          Neither approach is universally better for every application: indirect flat panels remain a practical choice for very large-area, cost-sensitive systems. Direct conversion is at its strongest where sharpness, contrast resolution and dose efficiency drive the application — such as intraoral dental imaging, high-speed scanning and demanding inline inspection.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- WHY LIGHT SPREAD MATTERS -->
    <section class="tech-section tech-section--light" id="light-spread">
      <div class="container container--narrow">
        <p class="section-eyebrow reveal">Signal Pathway</p>
        <h2 class="section-headline reveal">Why does scintillator light spread matter?</h2>
        <p class="tech-body reveal">
          In an indirect detector, each absorbed X-ray produces a small flash of visible light inside the scintillator. That light radiates in all directions, and part of it travels laterally before it reaches the photodiode layer below. The result is that the signal from a single X-ray photon can be recorded over a wider footprint than the point where the photon was actually absorbed — one of the mechanisms that limit sharpness in indirect systems. Columnar CsI scintillators channel light downwards to reduce this effect, but the light-conversion stage itself remains.
        </p>
        <p class="tech-body reveal">
          Direct conversion removes this specific stage. The photon's energy becomes electrical charge inside the semiconductor, and the applied electric field pulls that charge to the nearest pixel electrodes. The signal footprint stays closer to the absorption point — a shorter, more localized X-ray-to-signal pathway.
        </p>

        <div class="tech-pathway reveal" role="img" aria-label="Diagram comparing indirect and direct conversion. Left: in an indirect detector an X-ray creates visible light in the scintillator, and the light spreads laterally before reaching the photodiode array, producing a wide signal footprint. Right: in a direct conversion detector the X-ray creates charge in the semiconductor, and the charge is collected by the nearest pixel electrode, producing a narrow signal footprint.">
          <div class="tech-pathway-panel">
            <p class="tech-pathway-title">Indirect conversion</p>
            <svg viewBox="0 0 260 220" width="100%" aria-hidden="true">
              <line x1="130" y1="8" x2="130" y2="58" stroke="#43a1d8" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-dasharray="6 4" class="tech-anim-beam"/>
              <polygon points="130,64 125,52 135,52" fill="#43a1d8"/>
              <rect x="30" y="64" width="200" height="52" rx="4" fill="#f0b429" fill-opacity="0.14" stroke="#f0b429" stroke-opacity="0.5"/>
              <text x="36" y="80" class="tech-svg-label" fill="#a07c1c">Scintillator</text>
              <ellipse cx="130" cy="96" rx="12" ry="9" fill="#ffd86b" fill-opacity="0.9" class="tech-anim-cloud"/>
              <ellipse cx="130" cy="98" rx="36" ry="14" fill="#ffd86b" fill-opacity="0.45" class="tech-anim-cloud tech-anim-cloud--wide"/>
              <ellipse cx="130" cy="102" rx="66" ry="16" fill="#ffd86b" fill-opacity="0.2" class="tech-anim-cloud tech-anim-cloud--wider"/>
              <rect x="30" y="124" width="200" height="26" rx="4" fill="#5a6b76" fill-opacity="0.28" stroke="#5a6b76" stroke-opacity="0.55"/>
              <text x="36" y="141" class="tech-svg-label" fill="#5a6b76">Photodiode array</text>
              <path d="M64 162 q66 30 132 0" stroke="#c0392b" stroke-width="2" fill="none"/>
              <text x="130" y="196" text-anchor="middle" class="tech-svg-label" fill="#c0392b">Wide signal footprint</text>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <div class="tech-pathway-panel">
            <p class="tech-pathway-title">Direct conversion</p>
            <svg viewBox="0 0 260 220" width="100%" aria-hidden="true">
              <line x1="130" y1="8" x2="130" y2="58" stroke="#43a1d8" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-dasharray="6 4" class="tech-anim-beam"/>
              <polygon points="130,64 125,52 135,52" fill="#43a1d8"/>
              <rect x="30" y="64" width="200" height="52" rx="4" fill="#43a1d8" fill-opacity="0.12" stroke="#43a1d8" stroke-opacity="0.5"/>
              <text x="36" y="80" class="tech-svg-label" fill="#2b7bab">CdTe or Si semiconductor</text>
              <rect x="124" y="72" width="12" height="44" rx="3" fill="#43a1d8" fill-opacity="0.85" class="tech-anim-column"/>
              <rect x="30" y="124" width="200" height="26" rx="4" fill="#5a6b76" fill-opacity="0.28" stroke="#5a6b76" stroke-opacity="0.55"/>
              <text x="36" y="141" class="tech-svg-label" fill="#5a6b76">CMOS / ASIC readout</text>
              <path d="M116 162 q14 12 28 0" stroke="#2e8b57" stroke-width="2" fill="none"/>
              <text x="130" y="196" text-anchor="middle" class="tech-svg-label" fill="#2e8b57">Narrow signal footprint</text>
            </svg>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="tech-caption reveal">In an indirect detector (left), visible light spreads laterally in the scintillator before detection. In a direct conversion detector (right), charge is collected at local pixel electrodes near the absorption point.</p>

        <p class="tech-body reveal">
          Direct conversion does not mean zero blur: effects such as charge sharing between neighbouring pixels, K-fluorescence and system-level factors (focal spot, motion) still exist in any real detector. What direct conversion removes is the scintillator light-spread stage — a dominant source of signal spreading in indirect designs.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- HOW ATHLOS WORKS -->
    <section class="tech-section tech-section--dark" id="athlos-architecture">
      <div class="container">
        <p class="section-eyebrow section-eyebrow--light reveal">Athlos Architecture</p>
        <h2 class="section-headline section-headline--light reveal">How do Athlos direct conversion sensors work?</h2>
        <div class="tech-two-col">
          <div>
            <p class="tech-body tech-body--dark reveal">
              Athlos builds two direct conversion architectures on one common principle — detector material bonded directly to a full-custom CMOS/ASIC readout:
            </p>
            <p class="tech-body tech-body--dark reveal">
              <strong>CdTe-CMOS</strong> — cadmium telluride detector material bump-bonded to a proprietary full-custom ASIC. Used in the <a href="/products/ufs.html">Ultra-Fast Scanning Sensor (UFS)</a> and the <a href="/products/ufs-ip67.html">Industrial IP67 TDI</a>. CdTe's high atomic number gives strong absorption efficiency across a wide energy range, which suits scanning applications in dental, medical and industrial imaging.
            </p>
            <p class="tech-body tech-body--dark reveal">
              <strong>Si-CMOS</strong> — crystalline silicon detector material integrated with CMOS readout. Used in the <a href="/products/wios.html">DC-Air® wireless intraoral sensor</a>, where a compact sensor structure and high spatial resolution matter most in a size-constrained intraoral format.
            </p>
