    </section>

    <!-- CDTE VS SI -->
    <section class="tech-section tech-section--light" id="cdte-vs-si">
      <div class="container container--narrow">
        <p class="section-eyebrow reveal">Two Materials</p>
        <h2 class="section-headline reveal">CdTe-CMOS vs. Si-CMOS: which material for which job?</h2>
        <div class="tech-table-wrap reveal">
          <table class="tech-table tech-table--light">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col"></th>
                <th scope="col">CdTe-CMOS</th>
                <th scope="col">Si-CMOS</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Athlos products</th>
                <td><a href="/products/ufs.html">UFS</a>, <a href="/products/ufs-ip67.html">Industrial IP67 TDI</a></td>
                <td><a href="/products/wios.html">DC-Air®</a></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Absorption</th>
                <td>High-Z material — efficient across a wide energy range (Athlos scanning sensors: 10–360 kV)</td>
                <td>Efficient at intraoral dental energies; thin absorber supports compact designs</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Best suited for</th>
                <td>Line-scan / TDI scanning: panoramic and cephalometric dental, medical, inline industrial inspection</td>
                <td>Compact, high-resolution static imaging: intraoral dental</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Shared principle</th>
                <td colspan="2">Both convert X-rays directly to charge — no scintillator light-spread stage</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="tech-body reveal">
          Neither material is universally better; the choice depends on photon energies, geometry, speed and the form factor of the application.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- PHOTON COUNTING VS CHARGE INTEGRATION -->
    <section class="tech-section tech-section--dark" id="photon-counting">
      <div class="container container--narrow">
        <p class="section-eyebrow section-eyebrow--light reveal">Readout Modes</p>
        <h2 class="section-headline section-headline--light reveal">Is direct conversion the same as photon counting?</h2>
        <p class="tech-body tech-body--dark reveal">
          No — and the distinction matters. <strong>Direct conversion</strong> describes the <em>conversion pathway</em>: X-ray energy becomes electrical charge directly in the semiconductor. <strong>Photon counting</strong> and <strong>charge integration</strong> describe the <em>readout approach</em>: how the electronics turn that charge into image data.
        </p>
        <ul class="tech-list reveal">
          <li>A <strong>charge-integrating (energy-integrating)</strong> readout accumulates the charge from many photons over an exposure window and digitizes the total.</li>
          <li>A <strong>photon-counting</strong> readout compares each individual photon's pulse against a threshold and counts events one by one, optionally sorting them by energy.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="tech-note reveal">
          <p>
            <strong>Athlos sensors use direct conversion with charge-integrating readout.</strong> They are single-photon sensitive — the charge generated by a single X-ray photon is detectable above the noise floor — but they do not count individual photons or measure the energy of each photon separately. The UFS platform, for example, offers single-photon-sensitive TDI performance with a 16-bit dynamic range.
          </p>
        </div>
        <p class="tech-body tech-body--dark reveal">
          A direct conversion detector can be designed for either readout mode. The Simage/Ajat team has built both: charge-integrating direct conversion imagers (from the 1996 IEEE pixel device onwards) and a photon-counting camera published in <em>NIM&nbsp;A</em> in 2005. That photon-counting work is part of the team's documented design heritage — not a feature of current Athlos products.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- TDI -->
    <section class="tech-section tech-section--light" id="tdi">
      <div class="container container--narrow">
        <p class="section-eyebrow reveal">Scanning</p>
        <h2 class="section-headline reveal">What is TDI scanning?</h2>
        <p class="tech-body reveal">
          <strong>TDI (Time Delay Integration)</strong> is a line-scan technique in which the readout of a multi-row linear detector is synchronized with the motion of the object being scanned. As the object moves across the detector rows, each row adds its measurement of the same object slice; after N stages the signal has been accumulated N times, which improves signal-to-noise ratio without slowing the scan.
        </p>
        <div class="tech-tdi reveal" role="img" aria-label="Diagram of TDI scanning: an object moves across the detector's line stages, and each stage adds signal from the same object slice, so the accumulated signal grows stage by stage.">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 320 130" width="100%" aria-hidden="true">
            <rect x="16" y="18" width="40" height="24" rx="4" fill="#43a1d8" fill-opacity="0.55" class="tech-anim-object"/>
            <text x="36" y="12" text-anchor="middle" class="tech-svg-label" fill="#5a6b76">Object motion →</text>
            <g>
              <rect x="16" y="58" width="56" height="30" rx="3" fill="#43a1d8" fill-opacity="0.15" stroke="#43a1d8" stroke-opacity="0.5"/>
              <rect x="80" y="58" width="56" height="30" rx="3" fill="#43a1d8" fill-opacity="0.3" stroke="#43a1d8" stroke-opacity="0.5"/>
              <rect x="144" y="58" width="56" height="30" rx="3" fill="#43a1d8" fill-opacity="0.5" stroke="#43a1d8" stroke-opacity="0.6"/>
              <rect x="208" y="58" width="56" height="30" rx="3" fill="#43a1d8" fill-opacity="0.7" stroke="#43a1d8" stroke-opacity="0.7"/>
              <rect x="272" y="58" width="32" height="30" rx="3" fill="#43a1d8" fill-opacity="0.9" stroke="#43a1d8"/>
            </g>
            <text x="16" y="106" class="tech-svg-label" fill="#5a6b76">Stage 1</text>
            <text x="230" y="106" class="tech-svg-label" fill="#5a6b76">Stage N — accumulated signal</text>
            <text x="16" y="124" class="tech-svg-label" fill="#2b7bab">Signal accumulates as the object passes each TDI stage</text>
          </svg>
        </div>
        <p class="tech-body reveal">
          This is why TDI suits high-speed, dose-constrained scanning: conveyor-belt inline inspection, panoramic and cephalometric dental imaging, and continuous industrial NDT. The Athlos <a href="/products/ufs.html">Ultra-Fast Scanning Sensor</a> and <a href="/products/ufs-ip67.html">Industrial IP67 TDI</a> both operate in TDI mode with <strong>28 TDI stages</strong> and line rates up to 75,000 lines per second — enabling scan speeds from 28 m/min at 100 µm resolution up to 1,700 m/min at 400 µm.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- LINEAGE TIMELINE -->
    <section class="tech-section tech-section--dark" id="lineage">
      <div class="container container--narrow">
        <p class="section-eyebrow section-eyebrow--light reveal">Three Decades</p>
        <h2 class="section-headline section-headline--light reveal">Technology lineage: Simage → Ajat → Athlos</h2>
        <p class="tech-body tech-body--dark reveal">
          The Athlos team has worked on direct conversion X-ray imaging longer than almost anyone in the field. The same core group has carried the technology through three decades and three companies:
        </p>
        <ol class="tech-timeline reveal">
          <li>
            <span class="tech-timeline-year">1993</span>
            <p>Konstantinos Spartiotis, returning from his PhD at Johns Hopkins University and work at CERN, founds <strong>Simage Oy</strong> in Espoo, Finland — one of the earliest companies in the world to develop direct conversion X-ray imaging.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="tech-timeline-year">1996–1997</span>
            <p>Simage presents a semiconductor pixel imaging device and a direct digital radiology system at IEEE and CAR'97: CdZnTe and Si pixel detectors flip-chip bonded to CMOS ASICs, 35 µm pixels, tiled into large imaging areas — with first preclinical and clinical images.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="tech-timeline-year">1997</span>
            <p>Patent family WO 97/08751 ("Imaging system and method") is published. Simage is eventually granted dozens of patents in direct conversion, CdTe contact technology and photon counting.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="tech-timeline-year">2001</span>
            <p><strong>Ajat Oy</strong> is founded; it becomes the global standard in direct conversion CdTe-CMOS dental panoramic and cephalometric imaging, with thousands of units deployed worldwide.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="tech-timeline-year">2003</span>
            <p>Peer-reviewed publication of a directly converting high-resolution Si intraoral sensor (<em>NIM&nbsp;A</em>&nbsp;501) — the technological ancestor of today's DC-Air®.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="tech-timeline-year">2004</span>
            <p>Peer-reviewed CdTe real-time imaging sensor and system (<em>NIM&nbsp;A</em>&nbsp;527): 100 µm CdTe-CMOS hybrids at video frame rates, with solutions to CdTe polarization.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="tech-timeline-year">2005</span>
            <p>Peer-reviewed photon-counting CdTe gamma/X-ray camera (<em>NIM&nbsp;A</em>&nbsp;550), demonstrating the team's photon-counting ASIC design capability.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="tech-timeline-year">2009 / 2019</span>
            <p>Ajat is acquired by XCounter AB; the group later becomes part of Varex Imaging. The founding team's expertise continues independently.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="tech-timeline-year">2017 →</span>
            <p><strong>Athlos Oy</strong> is founded and carries the lineage forward with a new generation of direct conversion products: DC-Air®, the Ultra-Fast Scanning Sensor and the Industrial IP67 TDI — designed and manufactured in Finland.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p class="tech-body tech-body--dark reveal">
          These early systems were research and first-generation devices; their specifications are historical and do not describe current Athlos products. What has accumulated across the three companies is the know-how: detector material engineering, contact technology, bump bonding, ASIC design, calibration and image processing.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

